{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## RCA (Phase 1) + diagnosability fix (Phase 2 AC#3) — rebar-debug protocol\n\n**Investigator:** Copilot CLI (rebar-debug skill), fresh worktree off origin/main @ 9532650f24.\n\n### What is PROVEN (cited)\nThe incident's *shape and cost* — a 99%-then-52-min-silent job killed at the 60-min cap with\nno traceback and orphaned pytest+4 workers — is caused by a **missing per-test hang guard** in\nthe shared reusable CI suite. `.github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml` runs the xdist suite\n(`pytest ... -n 4 --dist worksteal -q`) with **no `--timeout`** and `pytest-timeout` is not even\na declared dependency. `timeout-minutes: 60` (job-level, asserted by the existing\n`test_shared_pytest_job_has_incident_timeout`) only bounds the WHOLE job, so a single test that\nblocks in a C-level syscall (flock / socket recv / subprocess pipe) hides behind the last `-q`\ndot and burns the full hour invisibly. This is a genuine defect against this ticket's AC#3\n(\"whatever makes the hang diagnosable … is committed\").\n\n### Discriminating runtime proof\nA minimal test that does a blocking `fcntl.flock(LOCK_EX)` with no timeout (the exact hang shape\nsuspected here), run under `-n 2 --dist worksteal`:\n- **without** a guard → hangs indefinitely; an outer 25s `timeout` had to SIGKILL it (exit 124)\n  — reproduces the incident's unbounded silence.\n- **with** `--timeout=3 --timeout-method=thread` → aborted in **3.36s** and the culprit test is\n  **named** (`… test_flock_deadlock_hangs_forever`), worker faulthandler-dumped.\n`--timeout-method=thread` is load-bearing: the default `signal`/SIGALRM method cannot fire while\na worker is blocked in a C call, so it would still go silent — proven by the flock probe.\n\n### What is NOT proven (honest)\nThe *specific* hanging test is **not** identified. It does not reproduce off the ubuntu/py3.11\nLinux runner: I ran the CI-faithful selection locally (`-n 4 --dist worksteal`, py3.12/macOS)\nunder `--timeout=90 --timeout-method=thread` — 15m20s, **zero** timeout-tripped hangs (matching\nthis ticket's own \"does not reproduce locally\"). Best-evidenced candidates (unconfirmed) sit in\nthe network/subprocess/lock-heavy tail: `fcntl.flock` write-lock paths in `rebar._store.lock`,\ntests shelling `rebar`/`git` as subprocesses, and the snapshot-fetch/review-bot drain paths.\nThe committed guard makes the NEXT recurrence name itself, closing AC#1 automatically.\n\n### Fix (validated locally; RED→GREEN; held for landing)\n- `.github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml`: add `--timeout=300 --timeout-method=thread` to BOTH\n  parallel pytest steps (default suite + integration tier). 300s dwarfs the slowest legitimate\n  test; a true hang now fails in 5 min with a stack, not 60 min silent.\n- `pyproject.toml` + `uv.lock`: declare `pytest-timeout==2.4.0` in the `dev` extra (CI installs it\n  via `uv sync --locked --extra dev`).\n- RED→GREEN guard test `tests/unit/test_ci_workflow_parity.py::\n  test_shared_pytest_suite_has_a_per_test_hang_guard`: asserts every parallel pytest step carries\n  `--timeout=` AND `--timeout-method=thread`. Confirmed RED first; GREEN after; mutation check —\n  removing either flag returns it to RED.\n\n### Status\nAC#3 satisfied (diagnosability instrument committed + guarded). AC#1/AC#2 require CI deployment\nand will resolve on the next run/recurrence. **Not landed** — the change must go through Gerrit\n(`git push gerrit HEAD:refs/for/main`); landing decision handed to the operator."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786131571391673001,"uuid":"53f24929-0b3a-4c56-af8f-d0e7cefe5fa0"}